  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-93",
              "name": "CVE-2026-45070 in symfony/mime 8.1.7 (email header injection, transitive via laravel/framework)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-45070 in symfony/mime 8.1.7 (email header injection, transitive via laravel/framework)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`composer.lock` pins `symfony/mime` **v8.1.7**, which is covered by CVE-2026-45070 (email header injection via non-token bytes in MIME parameter names). Fixed in v8.1.10 (also 5.4.55 / 6.4.49 / 7.4.22). The package is pulled in transitively by the direct dependency `laravel/framework` v13.38.0."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`composer.lock` pins `symfony/mime` **v8.1.7**, which is covered by CVE-2026-45070 (email header injection via non-token bytes in MIME parameter names). Fixed in v8.1.10 (also 5.4.55 / 6.4.49 / 7.4.22). The package is pulled in transitively by the direct dependency `laravel/framework` v13.38.0.\n\nIn the abstract, a caller that derives a MIME parameter name from untrusted input could inject CRLF/additional headers into outgoing mail. In this codebase no such caller exists: Laravel's mail layer never derives parameter names from request input, and the app's only notification is the password-reset mail, so the vulnerable path is not reachable.\n\nUpgrade laravel/framework to a release whose constraint resolves symfony/mime >= 8.1.10 (or add a composer conflict/require for `symfony/mime:>=8.1.10` and run `composer update symfony/mime`). No application code change is required; the vulnerable parameter-name path is unused.",
                "markdown": "`composer.lock` pins `symfony/mime` **v8.1.7**, which is covered by CVE-2026-45070 (email header injection via non-token bytes in MIME parameter names). Fixed in v8.1.10 (also 5.4.55 / 6.4.49 / 7.4.22). The package is pulled in transitively by the direct dependency `laravel/framework` v13.38.0.\n\nIn the abstract, a caller that derives a MIME parameter name from untrusted input could inject CRLF/additional headers into outgoing mail. In this codebase no such caller exists: Laravel's mail layer never derives parameter names from request input, and the app's only notification is the password-reset mail, so the vulnerable path is not reachable.\n\nUpgrade laravel/framework to a release whose constraint resolves symfony/mime >= 8.1.10 (or add a composer conflict/require for `symfony/mime:>=8.1.10` and run `composer update symfony/mime`). No application code change is required; the vulnerable parameter-name path is unused."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-93",
                  "CVE-2026-45070",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/93.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-863",
              "name": "CVE-2026-118514 in symfony/http-foundation 8.1.9 (HEAD bypass of method-scoped RequestMatcher rules, transitive via laravel/framework)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-118514 in symfony/http-foundation 8.1.9 (HEAD bypass of method-scoped RequestMatcher rules, transitive via laravel/framework)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`composer.lock` pins `symfony/http-foundation` **v8.1.9**, covered by CVE-2026-118514: `RequestMatcher`/`MethodRequestMatcher` compared the request method verbatim, so security rules scoped to GET could be bypassed with a HEAD request (incomplete fix of CVE-2026-45075). Fixed in v8.1.11 (also 5.4.56 / 6.4.50 / 7.4.23). Transitive dependency of the direct `laravel/framework` v13.38.0."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`composer.lock` pins `symfony/http-foundation` **v8.1.9**, covered by CVE-2026-118514: `RequestMatcher`/`MethodRequestMatcher` compared the request method verbatim, so security rules scoped to GET could be bypassed with a HEAD request (incomplete fix of CVE-2026-45075). Fixed in v8.1.11 (also 5.4.56 / 6.4.50 / 7.4.23). Transitive dependency of the direct `laravel/framework` v13.38.0.\n\nIn the abstract, method-scoped security rules (Symfony security access_control with a `methods` option) can be bypassed by sending HEAD instead of GET, reaching controller side effects without a response body. In this Laravel application no Symfony security RequestMatchers exist and authorization is enforced by Laravel middleware/role checks inside controllers, so the bypass has no target and the vulnerable path is not reachable.\n\nRun `composer update symfony/http-foundation` (or bump laravel/framework to a release resolving http-foundation >= 8.1.11). No application change required; no method-scoped matcher rules exist to be bypassed.",
                "markdown": "`composer.lock` pins `symfony/http-foundation` **v8.1.9**, covered by CVE-2026-118514: `RequestMatcher`/`MethodRequestMatcher` compared the request method verbatim, so security rules scoped to GET could be bypassed with a HEAD request (incomplete fix of CVE-2026-45075). Fixed in v8.1.11 (also 5.4.56 / 6.4.50 / 7.4.23). Transitive dependency of the direct `laravel/framework` v13.38.0.\n\nIn the abstract, method-scoped security rules (Symfony security access_control with a `methods` option) can be bypassed by sending HEAD instead of GET, reaching controller side effects without a response body. In this Laravel application no Symfony security RequestMatchers exist and authorization is enforced by Laravel middleware/role checks inside controllers, so the bypass has no target and the vulnerable path is not reachable.\n\nRun `composer update symfony/http-foundation` (or bump laravel/framework to a release resolving http-foundation >= 8.1.11). No application change required; no method-scoped matcher rules exist to be bypassed."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-863",
                  "CVE-2026-118514",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/863.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-346",
              "name": "Password reset link poisoning via Host header leads to account takeover",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Password reset link poisoning via Host header leads to account takeover"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The application trusts the client-supplied `Host` header when building password-reset links. An unauthenticated attacker can request a password reset for any account (instructor or cadet) with a spoofed `Host`/`X-Forwarded-Host` header, causing the emailed reset link to point at an attacker-controlled domain. When the recipient clicks it, the reset token is disclosed to the attacker, who uses it on the real site to set a new password and take over the account."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The application trusts the client-supplied `Host` header when building password-reset links. An unauthenticated attacker can request a password reset for any account (instructor or cadet) with a spoofed `Host`/`X-Forwarded-Host` header, causing the emailed reset link to point at an attacker-controlled domain. When the recipient clicks it, the reset token is disclosed to the attacker, who uses it on the real site to set a new password and take over the account.\n\nFull account takeover of any user, including instructor accounts, without knowing the current password. Once logged in as the instructor, the attacker gains the full instructor plane: creating/approving/deleting cadet accounts, impersonating any cadet, reading all cadet PNRs and activity logs, grading, and scenario management.\n\nDerive password-reset link origins from the configured application URL rather than the request. Apply the provided change (register a `ResetPassword::createUrlUsing` callback that builds the link from `config('app.url')`), and verify `APP_URL` is set to the real public origin on every deployment — the installer wizard already writes it during provisioning. Additionally, replace the blanket proxy trust in `bootstrap/app.php` (`trustProxies(at: '*')`) with the specific load-balancer/proxy IPs or header set in use, so `Host`/`X-Forwarded-*` headers are not accepted from arbitrary clients; this also hardens every other request-derived URL in the application.",
                "markdown": "The application trusts the client-supplied `Host` header when building password-reset links. An unauthenticated attacker can request a password reset for any account (instructor or cadet) with a spoofed `Host`/`X-Forwarded-Host` header, causing the emailed reset link to point at an attacker-controlled domain. When the recipient clicks it, the reset token is disclosed to the attacker, who uses it on the real site to set a new password and take over the account.\n\nFull account takeover of any user, including instructor accounts, without knowing the current password. Once logged in as the instructor, the attacker gains the full instructor plane: creating/approving/deleting cadet accounts, impersonating any cadet, reading all cadet PNRs and activity logs, grading, and scenario management.\n\nDerive password-reset link origins from the configured application URL rather than the request. Apply the provided change (register a `ResetPassword::createUrlUsing` callback that builds the link from `config('app.url')`), and verify `APP_URL` is set to the real public origin on every deployment — the installer wizard already writes it during provisioning. Additionally, replace the blanket proxy trust in `bootstrap/app.php` (`trustProxies(at: '*')`) with the specific load-balancer/proxy IPs or header set in use, so `Host`/`X-Forwarded-*` headers are not accepted from arbitrary clients; this also hardens every other request-derived URL in the application."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-346",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/346.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "Unauthenticated PNR disclosure via guest-exempt ownership check in terminal RT retrieval",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated PNR disclosure via guest-exempt ownership check in terminal RT retrieval"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated callers of the terminal API can retrieve any stored PNR by its 6-character record locator or by a passenger-surname search, bypassing the ownership check that applies to authenticated students. The check at AmadeusParser.php:2354 only rejects when the caller is a non-owner AND is not a guest, so the no-session case reads every record."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated callers of the terminal API can retrieve any stored PNR by its 6-character record locator or by a passenger-surname search, bypassing the ownership check that applies to authenticated students. The check at AmadeusParser.php:2354 only rejects when the caller is a non-owner AND is not a guest, so the no-session case reads every record.\n\nAnyone who can reach the app without credentials reads the full booking record of any cadet or instructor in the database: passenger names, itinerary segments, stored contact details (phone/email as entered), ticketing data and form of payment. The same guest identity can write queue items attributed to any office id. Cross-office confidentiality boundary of the classroom is void for this data type.\n\nRequire an authenticated session for terminal command execution (return 401 for guests in TerminalController::execute, matching the ET/TTP/queue branches that already refuse guests), and delete the `$userId !== 'guest'` exemption from the RT ownership check and from the surname-search scoping so no caller identity other than a same-office instructor can read stored PNRs they do not own. Reject or office-scope guest queue writes on the QE office-target path.",
                "markdown": "Unauthenticated callers of the terminal API can retrieve any stored PNR by its 6-character record locator or by a passenger-surname search, bypassing the ownership check that applies to authenticated students. The check at AmadeusParser.php:2354 only rejects when the caller is a non-owner AND is not a guest, so the no-session case reads every record.\n\nAnyone who can reach the app without credentials reads the full booking record of any cadet or instructor in the database: passenger names, itinerary segments, stored contact details (phone/email as entered), ticketing data and form of payment. The same guest identity can write queue items attributed to any office id. Cross-office confidentiality boundary of the classroom is void for this data type.\n\nRequire an authenticated session for terminal command execution (return 401 for guests in TerminalController::execute, matching the ET/TTP/queue branches that already refuse guests), and delete the `$userId !== 'guest'` exemption from the RT ownership check and from the surname-search scoping so no caller identity other than a same-office instructor can read stored PNRs they do not own. Reject or office-scope guest queue writes on the QE office-target path."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Cross-cadet PNR disclosure via unscoped single-letter PNR index (RT/A) and list-line retrieval",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cross-cadet PNR disclosure via unscoped single-letter PNR index (RT/A) and list-line retrieval"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The terminal's single-letter PNR index (`RT/A` … `RT/Z`) queries the 50 most recently updated PNRs with no user or office scoping, so any authenticated cadet receives the passenger names and record locators of classmates' bookings, and the follow-up `RT<LINE>` command returns the complete foreign PNR without any ownership check."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The terminal's single-letter PNR index (`RT/A` … `RT/Z`) queries the 50 most recently updated PNRs with no user or office scoping, so any authenticated cadet receives the passenger names and record locators of classmates' bookings, and the follow-up `RT<LINE>` command returns the complete foreign PNR without any ownership check.\n\nAny approved cadet reads the full training PNRs of every other cadet in the database (and instructors' records): passenger names, itineraries, contact phone/email elements, ticketing arrangement, received-from values, and form of payment via the full-record response. This defeats the per-user PNR isolation the direct RT<locator> path enforces, and exposes other students' practice work product to copying.\n\nApply the same caller scoping the surname-search branch already uses to the single-letter index branch: restrict the query to `user_id = $userId` for non-instructor callers (and additionally to the instructor's office for instructor callers if cross-office visibility is not intended). Also enforce ownership on the list-line retrieval paths (`RT<LINE>`, `LM/LP<LINE>`) by filtering `lastPnrList` to records the caller may read before display.",
                "markdown": "The terminal's single-letter PNR index (`RT/A` … `RT/Z`) queries the 50 most recently updated PNRs with no user or office scoping, so any authenticated cadet receives the passenger names and record locators of classmates' bookings, and the follow-up `RT<LINE>` command returns the complete foreign PNR without any ownership check.\n\nAny approved cadet reads the full training PNRs of every other cadet in the database (and instructors' records): passenger names, itineraries, contact phone/email elements, ticketing arrangement, received-from values, and form of payment via the full-record response. This defeats the per-user PNR isolation the direct RT<locator> path enforces, and exposes other students' practice work product to copying.\n\nApply the same caller scoping the surname-search branch already uses to the single-letter index branch: restrict the query to `user_id = $userId` for non-instructor callers (and additionally to the instructor's office for instructor callers if cross-office visibility is not intended). Also enforce ownership on the list-line retrieval paths (`RT<LINE>`, `LM/LP<LINE>`) by filtering `lastPnrList` to records the caller may read before display."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-918",
              "name": "Unauthenticated installer test-db endpoint: SSRF port-scan oracle, link-local blocklist bypass, and worker-exhaustion DoS",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated installer test-db endpoint: SSRF port-scan oracle, link-local blocklist bypass, and worker-exhaustion DoS"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The unauthenticated installer endpoint POST /install/test-db lets a remote attacker make the server open TCP connections to arbitrary internal hosts and ports. The link-local (cloud metadata) blocklist is bypassable with alternative IP literal notations (hex `0xa9fea9fe`, octal `0177.0.0.1`, trailing-dot hostnames), and any host/port that accepts a connection but stays silent pins the PHP worker indefinitely (PDO::ATTR_TIMEOUT does not bound the initial read). This yields both an SSRF/port-scanning oracle and a worker-exhaustion denial of service during the pre-provisioning window of every new deployment."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The unauthenticated installer endpoint POST /install/test-db lets a remote attacker make the server open TCP connections to arbitrary internal hosts and ports. The link-local (cloud metadata) blocklist is bypassable with alternative IP literal notations (hex `0xa9fea9fe`, octal `0177.0.0.1`, trailing-dot hostnames), and any host/port that accepts a connection but stays silent pins the PHP worker indefinitely (PDO::ATTR_TIMEOUT does not bound the initial read). This yields both an SSRF/port-scanning oracle and a worker-exhaustion denial of service during the pre-provisioning window of every new deployment.\n\nDuring the pre-provisioning window an unauthenticated attacker can (1) probe internal networks through the application server (distinguishing closed ports — instant 422 — from open services — hang or delayed failure), (2) reach link-local cloud metadata addresses despite the blocklist, and (3) with a handful of requests point every PHP worker at a host/port that accepts and never speaks, making the whole application unresponsive (demonstrated: one request wedged the deployment until restart). No data was modified and no metadata content was retrieved in testing.\n\nResolve the database host once, fail closed when resolution does not yield a valid IP, pin the PDO DSN to that resolved dotted IP address, and gate the connection on a bounded TCP pre-flight. Concretely: reject any host whose `gethostbyname()` result is not a valid IP (this eliminates hex/octal/trailing-dot reinterpretation because the driver never sees the raw string); run the existing link-local range check against the resolved address; and before constructing the PDO connection, open a socket to the resolved IP with a short connect timeout and read the first protocol byte with a short read timeout, failing the request when either step exceeds the bound (a real MySQL/MariaDB server sends its handshake packet first). Additionally, consider disabling the installer routes entirely once the lock file exists, so the pre-provisioning window is the only exposure.",
                "markdown": "The unauthenticated installer endpoint POST /install/test-db lets a remote attacker make the server open TCP connections to arbitrary internal hosts and ports. The link-local (cloud metadata) blocklist is bypassable with alternative IP literal notations (hex `0xa9fea9fe`, octal `0177.0.0.1`, trailing-dot hostnames), and any host/port that accepts a connection but stays silent pins the PHP worker indefinitely (PDO::ATTR_TIMEOUT does not bound the initial read). This yields both an SSRF/port-scanning oracle and a worker-exhaustion denial of service during the pre-provisioning window of every new deployment.\n\nDuring the pre-provisioning window an unauthenticated attacker can (1) probe internal networks through the application server (distinguishing closed ports — instant 422 — from open services — hang or delayed failure), (2) reach link-local cloud metadata addresses despite the blocklist, and (3) with a handful of requests point every PHP worker at a host/port that accepts and never speaks, making the whole application unresponsive (demonstrated: one request wedged the deployment until restart). No data was modified and no metadata content was retrieved in testing.\n\nResolve the database host once, fail closed when resolution does not yield a valid IP, pin the PDO DSN to that resolved dotted IP address, and gate the connection on a bounded TCP pre-flight. Concretely: reject any host whose `gethostbyname()` result is not a valid IP (this eliminates hex/octal/trailing-dot reinterpretation because the driver never sees the raw string); run the existing link-local range check against the resolved address; and before constructing the PDO connection, open a socket to the resolved IP with a short connect timeout and read the first protocol byte with a short read timeout, failing the request when either step exceeds the bound (a real MySQL/MariaDB server sends its handshake packet first). Additionally, consider disabling the installer routes entirely once the lock file exists, so the pre-provisioning window is the only exposure."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html"
            },
