                "text": "Enforce scenario ownership before delete"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "DELETE /api/instructor/scenarios/{id}",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "app/Http/Controllers/DashboardController.php"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 456,
                        "endLine": 456
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "$scenario = Scenario::find($id);\n        abort_unless(\n            $scenario !== null\n            && $scenario->created_by !== null\n            && ($scenario->created_by === $user->username || trim($scenario->created_by ?? '') === 'instructor' || $scenario->created_by === ''),\n            404\n        );\n\n        $deleted = $this->scenarios->deleteScenario($id);"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Resolve the scenario before deleting and reject with 404 unless the current instructor is the creator (or the row is shared seeded curriculum). Longer term, add an office dimension to scenarios so instructor listings and management can be scoped consistently with every other instructor operation.",
                "markdown": "Resolve the scenario before deleting and reject with 404 unless the current instructor is the creator (or the row is shared seeded curriculum). Longer term, add an office dimension to scenarios so instructor listings and management can be scoped consistently with every other instructor operation."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "819b15495138b1c1f774d593a51095f85ba8d40de4c7a8290b5b49406696f655"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "2.7",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "bce97a93754bac0eba97d4e730836b152e124105890f14dbe9487bf9cadf2e3a",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0007",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 2.7,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-08 16:54:15 UTC",
              "target": "http://127.0.0.1:8088",
              "endpoint": "DELETE /api/instructor/scenarios/{id}",
              "method": "DELETE",
              "cwe": "CWE-639",
              "impact": "An instructor at one office can destroy the curriculum of another office and, with it, every cadet's graded submission records for that scenario across all offices — irreversible loss of other classes' assessment data (submissions, auto-grade results, instructor feedback). Demonstrated live: a cross-office instructor deleted another office's scenario and its submission vanished.",
              "technical_analysis": "`DashboardController::deleteScenario` (:445-475) checks only `$user->role !== 'instructor'` and then calls `ScenarioService::deleteScenario($id)` (ScenarioService.php:285-296), which loads the scenario by primary key, deletes **all** `Submission` rows for that scenario id, and deletes the scenario — no `created_by` comparison and no office check (the Scenario model has no office column at all). Every sibling instructor operation enforces the office boundary by resolving the target through `where('office_id', $user->office_id)` (approve :257, reject :287, destroy :316, impersonate :353, spectate DashboardController:380-382, grade :487-491), making scenario deletion the sole cross-office mutation. The submission cascade means the integrity impact extends beyond the scenario object to students' graded coursework that the deleting instructor has no relationship to.",
              "remediation_steps": "Resolve the scenario before deleting and reject with 404 unless the current instructor is the creator (or the row is shared seeded curriculum). Longer term, add an office dimension to scenarios so instructor listings and management can be scoped consistently with every other instructor operation.",
              "counterevidence": "Strongest case against severity: scenarios are deliberately global — every student of every office drills on every scenario (`ScenarioService::getScenariosForStudent` at ScenarioService.php:16-46 has no office filter, and the seeder stamps `created_by => 'instructor'` on shared curriculum), so any instructor deleting any scenario could be argued as intended admin-like behavior. That would make this a product decision rather than a broken control. Counter: the codebase consistently treats office_id as the isolation boundary for every other instructor power (studentsApi, approve, reject, destroy, impersonate, spectate, grade, broadcast, reset — all office-scoped, verified live cross-office 404s), so scenario deletion is the sole unscoped mutation, and it silently destroys other offices' students' graded submissions, which no shared-curriculum design would intend. Instructor accounts are also not mutually trusted where it matters (the model isolates their cohorts). Rated low accordingly: real issue, limited blast radius.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that scenarios are intended to be office-private (per-office curriculum) would raise this to medium/high; conversely, a product statement that all instructors are trusted co-admins of a single global curriculum would reduce it to informational hardening.",
              "fix_verification": "Security closure: re-traced the patched controller — a non-creator instructor now fails `abort_unless` with 404 before ScenarioService::deleteScenario runs, so neither the scenario row nor the cascading Submission delete can execute for that caller; the creator path and the seeded-curriculum path are unchanged. Bypass review: sibling mutation routes checked — approve/reject/destroy/impersonate resolve the student with `where('office_id', $user->office_id)` (verified live: cross-office 404), gradeSubmissionApi checks submission user office, broadcast/reset take the office from the authenticated user, so no equivalent unscoped deletion remains. Preserved behavior: creator deletes own scenarios; seeded scenarios (`created_by = 'instructor'` from the seeder) remain manageable by any instructor, preserving shared-curriculum workflows; 404 (not 403) keeps scenario ids non-enumerable. Repository checks: code-trace only on the shared runtime (no restart/test run to avoid disrupting concurrent agents); hunk uses existing `abort_unless` style from the same controller; gate 5 flagged for reviewer to run `php artisan test --filter=Scenario`.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. As instructor A (office LON1A0980), create a scenario via POST /api/instructor/scenarios (201).\n2. Have a cadet submit against it via POST /api/dashboard/scenarios/{id}/submit (200).\n3. As a cadet, attempt DELETE /api/instructor/scenarios/{id}: rejected 403 (role gate works).\n4. As instructor B of a DIFFERENT office, issue DELETE /api/instructor/scenarios/{id}: returns 200 and the scenario is deleted.\n5. Query the submissions table for that scenario id: zero rows remain — other-office students' graded work was destroyed by a foreign-office instructor.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-918",
          "ruleIndex": 5,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unauthenticated installer test-db endpoint: SSRF port-scan oracle, link-local blocklist bypass, and worker-exhaustion DoS\n\nThe unauthenticated installer endpoint POST /install/test-db lets a remote attacker make the server open TCP connections to arbitrary internal hosts and ports. The link-local (cloud metadata) blocklist is bypassable with alternative IP literal notations (hex `0xa9fea9fe`, octal `0177.0.0.1`, trailing-dot hostnames), and any host/port that accepts a connection but stays silent pins the PHP worker indefinitely (PDO::ATTR_TIMEOUT does not bound the initial read). This yields both an SSRF/port-scanning oracle and a worker-exhaustion denial of service during the pre-provisioning window of every new deployment."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "Amadus GDS Training Simulator (Laravel 13) — /install/test-db",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "cd3b23d64214689c0c7c8f24d0569ac64e3aa343048b236eee65ec22b05e3354"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "8e76cc1e2f109df99cd2de80962d67fea5f4a7eec1f197e83c21bce155a79ea6",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0008",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-08 17:17:47 UTC",
              "target": "Amadus GDS Training Simulator (Laravel 13) — /install/test-db",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-918",
              "impact": "During the pre-provisioning window an unauthenticated attacker can (1) probe internal networks through the application server (distinguishing closed ports — instant 422 — from open services — hang or delayed failure), (2) reach link-local cloud metadata addresses despite the blocklist, and (3) with a handful of requests point every PHP worker at a host/port that accepts and never speaks, making the whole application unresponsive (demonstrated: one request wedged the deployment until restart). No data was modified and no metadata content was retrieved in testing.",
              "technical_analysis": "`InstallerService::testDatabase()` (app/Services/Installer/InstallerService.php) guards the MySQL branch with a link-local check, but the guard is applied to the raw host string via `FILTER_VALIDATE_IP` + `gethostbyname`:\n\n```php title=app/Services/Installer/InstallerService.php startLineNumber=221 endLineNumber=233\n$hostToCheck = trim($host, ' []');\n$ip = filter_var($hostToCheck, FILTER_VALIDATE_IP) ? $hostToCheck : gethostbyname($hostToCheck);\n$packed = @inet_pton((string) $ip);\nif ($packed !== false) {\n    // Unwrap IPv4-mapped IPv6 (::ffff:169.254.x.x, incl. ::ffff:a9fe:a9fe).\n    if (strlen($packed) === 16 && str_starts_with($packed, str_repeat(\"\\0\", 10).\"\\xff\\xff\")) {\n        $packed = substr($packed, 12);\n    }\n    // 169.254.0.0/16 link-local range.\n    if (strlen($packed) === 4 && str_starts_with($packed, \"\\xa9\\xfe\")) {\n        return ['success' => false, 'error' => 'Connection to link-local metadata addresses is forbidden.'];\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`FILTER_VALIDATE_IP` accepts only canonical notations, so `0xa9fea9fe` (hex) and `169.254.169.254.` (trailing dot, fails `gethostbyname` on this stack) fall through the guard, and `0177.0.0.1` (single-octal) resolves to loopback without ever being range-checked. The unvalidated string then flows into the DSN:\n\n```php title=app/Services/Installer/InstallerService.php startLineNumber=237 endLineNumber=241\n$dsnNoDb = \"mysql:host={$host};port={$port};charset=utf8mb4\";\n$pdo = new PDO($dsnNoDb, $username, $password, [\n    PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE => PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION,\n    PDO::ATTR_TIMEOUT => 4,\n]);\n```\n\nMySQL clients resolve hex/octal/short-form literals themselves, so the connection lands on `169.254.169.254` (or any internal host) even though the check never saw that address — a check-vs-connect differential. Separately, `PDO::ATTR_TIMEOUT` bounds connect() but not the handshake read: a port that accepts and never sends leaves `mysqlnd` blocked on the initial read past the 4-second timeout. The generic catch already masks the driver message, but the *timing* remains a reliable oracle — ~0.04s 422 for a refused connection versus an effectively unbounded request for an open port. Under `php artisan serve` (the README's documented deployment, single worker), one such request wedges every route until the process is restarted. The endpoint is reachable unauthenticated during the pre-provisioning window on every fresh deployment; CSRF is not a barrier since the token is issued by the same open wizard page.",
              "remediation_steps": "Resolve the database host once, fail closed when resolution does not yield a valid IP, pin the PDO DSN to that resolved dotted IP address, and gate the connection on a bounded TCP pre-flight. Concretely: reject any host whose `gethostbyname()` result is not a valid IP (this eliminates hex/octal/trailing-dot reinterpretation because the driver never sees the raw string); run the existing link-local range check against the resolved address; and before constructing the PDO connection, open a socket to the resolved IP with a short connect timeout and read the first protocol byte with a short read timeout, failing the request when either step exceeds the bound (a real MySQL/MariaDB server sends its handshake packet first). Additionally, consider disabling the installer routes entirely once the lock file exists, so the pre-provisioning window is the only exposure.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively looked for mitigations: the guard already blocks dotted, decimal (2852039162), octal-dotted (0251.0376.0251.0376), and IPv4-mapped IPv6 (::ffff:169.254.169.254) forms — those stay blocked. The endpoint is throttle:10,1 and CSRF-protected, which slows scanning but does not prevent it and does not bound the hang. The generic error message already suppresses host/port reflection, so the oracle is timing/hang-based rather than message-based — real, but noisier than a reflective oracle. The lock disables the endpoint post-install, so exposure is the pre-provisioning window. On FPM/Apache deployments each hung request consumes a full worker/process, so impact is worker-exhaustion DoS, not necessarily total outage of a large pool.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrated retrieval of metadata content or a speaking internal service response through the endpoint (for example a MySQL server that reveals version strings or accepts attacker-chosen credentials) would raise Confidentiality and potentially severity to High; conversely, confirmation that deployments are never exposed on shared networks before an operator completes the wizard would lower it.",
              "fix_verification": "Gates run on a throwaway copy of the checkout (never the shared runtime):\n\n1. Applicability: `php -l` clean on both the repo and lab copy; diff contains only the resolution block, the pre-flight block, and the pinned DSN.\n2. Security closure: re-ran the full exploit matrix against the patched service — dotted/decimal/IPv4-mapped forms still return the link-local block; `0xa9fea9fe`, `0177.0.0.1`, `169.254.169.254.` now fail before PDO (`\"Database host could not be resolved to an IP address.\"`); silent-open port returns 422 in 2.05s and the app stays responsive.\n3. Bypass review: alternate input classes tried — `[::ffff:a9fe:a9fe]` bracketed mapped form, decimal `2852039162`, dotted-octal `0251.0376.0251.0376`, trailing dot — all blocked or fail-closed. The range check now also inspects the same `$resolvedIp` the DSN uses, removing the check-vs-connect differential entirely.\n4. Preserved behavior: full legitimate install through a real MariaDB (127.0.0.1:3306) completes — test-db 200, database created, migrations + seed run, instructor provisioned, lock written, `.env` updated; hostname input (`localhost`) still works via resolution; sqlite path untouched.\n5. Repository checks: `php artisan test` on the patched copy — 169 passed, 1170 assertions (includes InstallerTest 18/18 and SecurityHardeningTest). Gap: could not run tests against a production FPM/CloudLinux target (sandbox lacks one); behavior-verification used `php artisan serve`, matching the README's documented deployment mode.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Identify a deployment that has not completed the install wizard (no storage/installed lock): GET /install returns the wizard with HTTP 200.\n2. Obtain the CSRF token from the wizard page's meta tag and a session cookie.\n3. Closed-port control: POST /install/test-db with driver=mysql, host=127.0.0.1, port=1 — returns 422 in ~0.04s.\n4. Open-port probe: POST /install/test-db with the same form but port set to any TCP port that is open internally — the worker hangs for the remainder of the request (curl --max-time 12 gives up while the server-side read continues), giving a clean open/closed oracle.\n5. Link-local bypass: POST /install/test-db with host=0xa9fea9fe (hex for 169.254.169.254) — the guard does not recognize the form, the server opens the connection (a listener at 169.254.169.254:3306 logged the inbound TCP connect), proving the metadata range is reachable despite the blocklist.\n6. DoS: repeat step 4 against a port that accepts and never sends data; each request pins a worker indefinitely — on the documented `php artisan serve` deployment a single request makes every route time out.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
